<h2>The anatomy of a captured acquisition</h2>
<p>The chronology told its own story. On 21 September 1984 — three days before the amended Section 3(f)(vi) of the Land Acquisition Act, 1894 made acquisition for cooperative housing societies a "public purpose" — the society entered into an agreement with a private concern, Bhagya Enterprises. The enterprise’s task under the agreement was to ensure that land was acquired for the society, and its commission was pegged to the acquisition reaching the statutory milestones: the Section 4(1) notification and the Section 6 declaration. Government approval for acquiring 97 acres followed on 13 November 1987; the Section 4(1) notification of 22 November 1988 covered about 79 acres; the Section 6 declaration of 4 December 1989 brought the net down to roughly 59 acres.</p>
<p>The Supreme Court rejected outright the society’s characterisation of the enterprise as a mere service provider. An agent remunerated by results, whose results were government notifications, was — in the Court’s words — <strong>"a sure manifestation of the attempted fraud."</strong></p>

<h2>No scheme, no valid purpose</h2>
<p>Acquisition for a housing society presupposes a housing scheme approved by the Government — the actual scheme, meaning the layout and blueprint of what is to be built. None was ever placed before the Government. The scrutiny committees had been careless, and the 1987 approval was a cryptic order not even expressed in the name of the Governor as the applicable 1977 Rules required. The Court also found the compensation mechanics tainted: the society deposited only part of the balance, and the authorities accepted about Rs 19.6 lakh paid directly to landowners in place of a full statutory deposit — a course the Act does not permit. The presumption that possession had been taken was rebutted by the society’s own documents. A government-appointed committee’s findings completed the picture: missing membership records, large payments to agents, and statements of office bearers about bribes.</p>

<h2>Fraud against finality</h2>
<p>The landowners had won before a Single Judge of the Karnataka High Court, who found fraud, quashed the acquisition and even initiated contempt over a false affidavit. A Division Bench reversed on two classic shields: delay, and res judicata founded on an earlier decision that had upheld the same acquisition. The Supreme Court dismantled both. The earlier ruling had <em>presumed</em> an approved scheme without calling for the records, and the incriminating agreement was never before that court; the dismissal of a special leave petition against it in limine gave it no precedential weight. Invoking the settled principle that fraud vitiates all solemn acts, the Court held that the objections of delay and res judicata <strong>"pale into insignificance"</strong> once the fraud and undue influence stood established.</p>

<h2>The directions</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>1. Acquisition quashed</h4><p>The appeals were allowed and the Single Judge’s order restored; contempt proceedings were dropped only because of the time elapsed.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>2. Costs enhanced</h4><p>Costs were raised to Rs 50,000 for each petitioner or set of legal representatives.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>3. Possession restored</h4><p>Possession is to be restored to all landowners — including owners who had never challenged the acquisition themselves.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>4. Refund of compensation</h4><p>Owners must refund the compensation received from the Government, without interest; sums privately received need not be returned. The society has no claim over the lands.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>5. Unclaimed land auctioned</h4><p>Land not reclaimed is to be auctioned by the revenue authorities under the supervision of a judicial officer of at least District Judge (Selection Grade) rank, the proceeds going to victim compensation as directed through the High Court and the State Legal Services Authority.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for property litigation</h2>
<p>Three features of the judgment travel well beyond Bengaluru. First, the <strong>paper trail decides fraud cases</strong>: the success-fee agreement, the absent layout plan and the irregular deposit entries did the work that oral allegations never could. Practitioners challenging a tainted acquisition should reconstruct the statutory file step by step — approval, notification, declaration, award, deposit, possession — and identify which document is missing or impossible. Second, <strong>res judicata is not armour for fraud</strong>: a prior decision that never examined the fabricated foundation does not bar a later challenge that does. Third, the remedial package — restoration even to non-litigating owners, refund without interest, judicially supervised auction of unclaimed parcels — shows the breadth of relief available under Article 142 once a court concludes that the acquisition should never have happened.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>For landowning families sitting on decades-old grievances about acquisitions that produced no public project, the decision is a reminder that the enquiry is document-led. Certified copies of the acquisition file, the scheme approval and the treasury deposits are where such a challenge begins — and sometimes ends.</p>
</div>
